Types ( edit ) Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions are divided into two types . Intravascular haemolysis ( edit ) The most common cause of this reaction is ABO blood groups incompatibility , secondary to human errors . In this process , the donor 's red blood cells are destroyed by recipient 's preformed antibodies through the activation of complement system . Thus , the donor 's red blood cells break open and releases haemoglobin into the blood . The haemoglobin is then excreted into urine , causes haemoglobinuria . Meanwhile , haemoglobin can also pass through the liver system and process into bilirubin , which accumulates under the skin , causing jaundice . The release of red blood cell debris and the activation of complement system causes uncontrolled clotting ( disseminated intravascular coagulation ) and circulatory shock . Extravascular haemolysis ( edit ) In this case , the donor 's red blood cells are recognised and bound to IgG antibodies .","entities":["intravascular haemolysis","extravascular haemolysis","ABO blood groups incompatibility","human errors","preformed antibodies","complement system","haemoglobin","urine","haemoglobinuria","liver system","red blood cell debris","disseminated intravascular coagulation","circulatory shock","IgG antibodies","Red blood cells"],"facts":["Red blood cells -RELATED_TO-> Blood circulation","Red blood cells -RELATED_TO-> Liver","Generalized bleeding -RELATED_TO-> disseminated intravascular coagulation","Macrophages -RELATED_TO-> IgG antibodies","Red blood cells -RELATED_TO-> Spleen"]}
