1925 Record label Paramount Records Artist Sunset Four Jubilee Singers Genre Negro spiritual `` Wade in the Water '' ( Roud 5439 ) is the name of a Negro spiritual first published in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers ( 1901 ) by John Wesley Work II and his brother , Frederick J. Work ( see Fisk Jubilee Singers ) . It is associated with the songs of the Underground Railroad . Wade in the water Wade in the water , children Wade in the water God 's gon na trouble the water Contents ( hide ) 1 Meaning 2 In popular culture 3 Selective list of recordings 4 References 4.1 Sources 5 External links Meaning ( edit ) The song relates to both the Old and New Testaments . The verses reflect the Israelites ' escape out of Egypt as found in Exodus 14 . The chorus refers to healing : see John 5 : 4 , `` For an angel went down at a certain season into the pool , and troubled the water : whosoever then first after the troubling of the water stepped in was made whole of whatsoever disease he had . ''","entities":["Fisk Jubilee Singers","Paramount Records","Sunset Four Jubilee Singers","Negro spiritual","New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers","John Wesley Work II","Frederick J. Work","Israelites' escape out of Egypt","Exodus 14","healing","John 5:4","Wade in the Water","Underground Railroad","New Testament","Egypt"],"facts":["Wade in the Water -RELATED_TO-> Roud 5439","Paramount Records -RELATED_TO-> Good News Chariot's Coming and Wade in the Water","Wade in the Water -RELATED_TO-> American folk song","Sunset Four Jubilee Singers -RELATED_TO-> Good News Chariot's Coming and Wade in the Water","Wade in the Water -RELATED_TO-> Wade in the Water, Children"]}
