[Edd2638] {"title":"Beijing","text":"Beijing
And under the Hongwu Emperor 's feudal policies it was given to Zhu Di , one of his sons , who was created `` Prince of Yan '' . The early death of Zhu Yuanzhang 's heir led to a succession struggle on his death , one that ended with the victory of Zhu Di and the declaration of the new Yongle era . Since his harsh treatment of the Ming capital Yingtian ( modern Nanjing ) alienated many there , he established his fief as a new co-capital . The city of Beiping became Beijing ( northern capital ) or Shuntian in 1403 . The construction of the new imperial residence , the Forbidden City , took from 1406 to 1420 ; this period was also responsible for several other of the modern city 's major attractions , such as the Temple of Heaven and Tian'anmen . On 28 October 1420 , the city was officially designated the capital of the Ming dynasty in the same year that the Forbidden City was completed . Beijing became the empire 's primary capital and Yingtian , also called Nanjing ( southern capital ) , became the co-capital .","entities":["Ming dynasty","Hongwu Emperor","Zhu Di","Prince of Yan","Succession struggle","Victory of Zhu Di","Yongle era","Forbidden City","Construction of the Forbidden City","Official designation of Beijing as the capital of the Ming dynasty","Beiping became Beijing","Nanjing","Tian'anmen","Temple of Heaven","Beijing"],"facts":["Ming dynasty -RELATED_TO-> paper currency","Ming dynasty -RELATED_TO-> private foreign commerce","Ming dynasty -RELATED_TO-> privatization","Ming dynasty -RELATED_TO-> silver","Ming dynasty -LOCATED_IN-> China"]}
