The last death sentence was given in August 1984 , when Brenda Hodge was sentenced to death by Western Australia ( and subsequently had her sentence commuted to life imprisonment ) . Contents ( hide ) 1 History 1.1 Commonwealth 1.2 New South Wales 1.3 Victoria 1.4 Queensland 1.5 Western Australia 1.6 South Australia 1.7 Tasmania 1.8 Australian Capital Territory 1.9 Northern Territory 2 Public opinion 3 References 4 External links History ( edit ) Death sentences were carried out under Aboriginal customary law , either directly or through sorcery . In some cases the condemned could be denied mortuary rites . The first executions carried out under European law in Australia took place in Western Australia in 1629 , when Dutch authorities hanged the mutineers of the Batavia . Capital punishment had been part of the legal system of Australia since British settlement and during the 19th century , crimes that could carry a death sentence included burglary , sheep stealing , forgery , sexual assaults , murder and manslaughter , and there is one reported case of someone being executed for `` being illegally at large '' .","entities":["Australia","life imprisonment","British settlement","Sorcery","Brenda Hodge","Brenda Hodge sentenced to death","Brenda Hodge's sentence commuted to life imprisonment","Aboriginal customary law","mortuary rites","Executions of the Batavia mutineers","Dutch authorities","mutineers of the Batavia","Batavia","legal system of Australia","death sentence"],"facts":["Australia -PART_OF-> Cairns Group","Australia -PART_OF-> British Empire","Australia -PART_OF-> Northern Territory","Australia -PART_OF-> Tasmania","Australia -PART_OF-> Jervis Bay Territory"]}
