1 Large size 3.3. 2 Small size 4 Visual snapshot 5 Usage 5.1 Lockheed U-2 5.2 Incidents 6 Uncut currency sheets 7 References 8 External links Denomination overview ( edit ) The denomination of two dollars was authorized under a congressional act , and first used in March 1862 . The denomination was continuously used until the 1960s ; by this time the United States Note was the only remaining class of U.S. currency the two - dollar bill was assigned to . In 1966 it was decided to discontinue all United States Notes from production , which included the two - dollar bill . The two - dollar bill initially was not reassigned to the Federal Reserve Note class of United States currency and was thus fully discontinued ; the Treasury Department cited the two - dollar bill 's low use and unpopularity as the reason for not immediately resuming use of the denomination .","entities":["congressional act","United States Note","U.S. currency","Federal Reserve Note","Treasury Department","First use of the two-dollar denomination","Decision to discontinue United States Notes from production","United States two-dollar bill"],"facts":["U.S. currency -RELATED_TO-> United States","Large denominations of United States currency -RELATED_TO-> United States Note","United States two-dollar bill -RELATED_TO-> United States Note","$2 United States Note -RELATED_TO-> United States Note","United States two-dollar bill -RELATED_TO-> U.S. currency"]}
[E9657aa] {"title":"United States two-dollar bill","text":"United States two-dollar bill
Although they are generally available at most banks , two - dollar bills are usually not handed out arbitrarily except upon specific request by the customer , and even then , may cause a delay with a trip to the vault . Rarity ( edit ) The seeming rarity of a $2 bill can be attributed to its low printing numbers as a Federal Reserve Note . Hoarding of the series due to lack of public knowledge of the $2 bill has resulted in very few bills seen in circulation . After its initial release , supplies of the Series 1976 two - dollar bill were allowed to dwindle until August 1996 when a new series dated 1995 began to be printed . This series was only printed for the Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta . Today , there is a common misconception by the general public that the $2 bill is no longer being produced . According to the Treasury , it `` receives many letters asking why the $2 bill is no longer in circulation '' .","entities":[],"facts":[]}
[Ef67c4c] {"title":"United States two-dollar bill","text":"United States two-dollar bill
Each note was an obligation of the issuing Federal Reserve Bank and could only be redeemed at the corresponding bank . The obverse of the note featured a borderless portrait of Thomas Jefferson to left and wording in the entire center . The reverse featured a World War I battleship . Beginning in the 1950s , production of $2 bills began to decrease . The relative scarcity of the bills lead some to start saving any they found , with the inevitable result that the notes became rarer in circulation . Currently , the circulation of $2 bills accounts for around 1 % of the U.S. currency in circulation . Small size notes ( edit ) ( 6.14 × 2.61 in ≅ 156 × 66 mm ) 1928 -- 1966 ( edit ) In 1928 , when all U.S. currency was changed to its current size , the $2 bill was issued only as a United States Note . The obverse featured a cropped version of Thomas Jefferson 's portrait that had been on previous $2 bills . The reverse featured Jefferson 's home , Monticello . The note 's seal and serial numbers were red .","entities":["United States","World War I","United States two-dollar bill","United States Note","U.S. currency","Federal Reserve Bank","Monticello","Thomas Jefferson"],"facts":["United States -PART_OF-> Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Northern America","United States -PART_OF-> these ten countries","United States -PART_OF-> The Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Beekeeping by countries"]}
[E5f9eb2] {"title":"United States two-dollar bill","text":"United States two-dollar bill
On April 13 , 1976 , the Treasury Department reintroduced the $2 bill as a cost - saving measure . Series 1976 $2 bills were redesigned and issued as a Federal Reserve Note . The obverse design remains basically unchanged since 1928 and features the same portrait of Jefferson . A green treasury seal and serial numbers replace the red used on the previous United States Notes . Since the reissue of the bill coincided with the United States Bicentennial , it was decided to use a bicentennial themed design on the reverse . An engraved rendition ( not an exact reproduction ) of John Trumbull 's The Declaration of Independence replaced Monticello on the reverse . First day issues of the new $2 bills could be taken to a post office and stamped with the date `` APR 13 1976 '' . In all , 590,720,000 notes from Series 1976 were printed . Currently , stamped series 1976 $2 bills typically trade for about twice their face value . If the bills were stamped in an exotic place , the value may be slightly higher .","entities":["Jefferson","John Trumbull","United States two-dollar bill","The Declaration of Independence","Federal Reserve Note","Treasury Department","Monticello","Reintroduction of the $2 bill","United States Notes","United States Bicentennial","Post office"],"facts":["Jefferson -RELATED_TO-> John Adams administration","Jefferson -RELATED_TO-> Alien and Sedition Acts of 1798","Jefferson -RELATED_TO-> Republican leaders","Jefferson -RELATED_TO-> Jefferson campaign","Jefferson -RELATED_TO-> bill of rights"]}
