The largest recorded specimen found washed up on the shore of Massachusetts Bay in 1870 , had a bell with a diameter of 2.3 metres ( 7 ft 6 in ) and tentacles 37.0 m ( 121.4 ft ) long . Lion 's mane jellyfish have been observed below 42 ° N latitude for some time in the larger bays of the east coast of the United States . The lion 's mane jellyfish uses its stinging tentacles to capture , pull in and eat prey such as fish , sea creatures and smaller jellyfish . Contents ( hide ) 1 Taxonomy 2 Description 3 Ecology 4 Behavior and reproduction 5 Sting and human contact 6 In popular culture 7 Gallery 8 References 8.1 Other sources 9 External links Taxonomy ( edit ) Cyanea sp . The taxonomy of the Cyanea species is not fully agreed upon ; some zoologists have suggested that all species within the genus should be treated as one .","entities":["United States","East Coast of the United States","fish","sea creatures","Massachusetts Bay","Largest recorded specimen found washed up","largest recorded specimen","42 ° N latitude","Cyanea","smaller jellyfish","lion's mane jellyfish"],"facts":["United States -PART_OF-> Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Northern America","United States -PART_OF-> these ten countries","United States -PART_OF-> The Americas","United States -PART_OF-> Beekeeping by countries"]}
